Description: Quit the splash as usual when a session is slow to register
 Once a session registers, GDM waits 10 seconds for it to register its
 display too, which quits the splash and leaves its last frame for the
 session to paint over. If that hasn't come, a fallback quits the splash
 with a plain "plymouth quit", and Plymouth takes the screen back to the
 first VT. GDM reads that as someone switching to an empty VT and starts a
 login screen there, over the session that's running. GNOME Shell registers
 its display at once, so it never gets here. GNOME Kiosk doesn't, and
 gdm-wayland-session registers for it after the same 10 seconds, so the two
 race. PatroneOS's disc runs its installer in GNOME Kiosk, and about one boot
 in seven showed a login screen in its place. The session has the screen by
 then, so the fallback now quits the splash as the usual path does.
Author: William Daugherty <31995458+billsecond@users.noreply.github.com>
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-30
---
--- a/daemon/gdm-manager.c
+++ b/daemon/gdm-manager.c
@@ -202,7 +202,10 @@
         manager->plymouth_quit_timeout_id = 0;
 
         if (manager->plymouth_is_running) {
-                plymouth_quit_without_transition ();
+                /* The session has the screen by now, and a plain quit would
+                 * take it back to Plymouth's VT, where GDM would start a
+                 * login screen over the session. */
+                plymouth_quit_with_transition ();
                 manager->plymouth_is_running = FALSE;
         }
 }
